2026-10-28T17:39:44.8664253Z {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgV3XpfwmU8GqDw98sZNq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RNUyrH3ndxEuY6aNdbmo5c","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/gh-aw/agent/issue.json"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6008,"cache_read_input_tokens":24863,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6008,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2884b6b0-3966-4179-951c-301477a1d4f2","uuid":"bd615fa6-cc6f-427d-bbc9-0ec851d01492","timestamp":"2026-10-28T17:39:44.863Z","request_id":"req_011CgV3XpDuRPAmQd5a7RwBo"}
2026-10-28T17:39:44.8921275Z {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RNUyrH3ndxEuY6aNdbmo5c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"number\": 30,\n3\t  \"url\": \"https://github.com/wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent/issues/30\",\n4\t  \"repo\": \"wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent\",\n5\t  \"title\": \"[Bug]: aws_lb_listener silently ignores tcp_idle_timeout_seconds on TCP_UDP listeners\",\n6\t  \"body\": \"### Terraform and AWS Provider Version\\n\\n```console\\nTerraform v1.15.2\\non darwin_arm64\\n\\n\\nRuns execute in HCP Terraform, so the provider version is resolved there rather than pinned in the\\nconfiguration.\\n\\nThe Terraform CLI version is incidental: the behaviour is in provider code, and is present in every\\nrelease since the argument was introduced. `tcp_idle_timeout_seconds` first shipped in **v5.73.0**\\n(commit `0b605cb6eca`), and `listenerTypesSupported` has listed only `ProtocolEnumTcp` and\\n`ProtocolEnumGeneve` in v5.73.0, v6.0.0, v6.64.0 and current `main` (`9c81245db08`).\\n`ProtocolEnumTcpUdp` does not appear in the file in any of those.\\n```\\n\\n### Affected Resource(s) or Data Source(s)\\n\\n* `aws_lb_listener`\\n\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nSetting `tcp_idle_timeout_seconds` on a Network Load Balancer listener with `protocol = \\\"TCP_UDP\\\"`\\nconfigures the listener's `tcp.idle_timeout.seconds` attribute, as the AWS API and console both\\nallow for that listener type.\\n\\n\\n### Actual Behavior\\n\\nThe argument is silently ignored. No diff is planned, no error or warning is emitted, and the\\nlistener keeps the default idle timeout of 350 seconds. Nothing in the plan or apply output indicates\\nthat the configured value had no effect.\\n\\nBecause the provider does not read the attribute back for these listeners, a value set out of band is\\nalso not reported as drift \\u2014 so the argument is inert in both directions. Confirmed by doing exactly\\nthat: after setting the attribute to `6000` on a `TCP_UDP` listener in the console, a subsequent\\n`plan`/`apply` in the same workspace neither reverted the value nor reported any difference, while the\\nsame workspace did successfully manage the attribute on its `TCP` listeners.\\n\\n\\n### Relevant Error/Panic Output\\n\\nNone, and that is the substance of the report: no error, warning, or diff is produced. The\\nconfiguration is accepted and does nothing.\\n\\n\\n### Sample Terraform Configuration\\n\\n<details open>\\n<summary>Click to expand configuration</summary>\\n\\n```hcl\\nresource \\\"aws_lb\\\" \\\"test\\\" {\\n  name               = \\\"example-nlb\\\"\\n  load_balancer_type = \\\"network\\\"\\n  internal           = true\\n  subnets            = var.subnets\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_lb_target_group\\\" \\\"test\\\" {\\n  name     = \\\"example-tg\\\"\\n  port     = 514\\n  protocol = \\\"TCP_UDP\\\"\\n  vpc_id   = var.vpc_id\\n\\n  health_check {\\n    port     = 514\\n    protocol = \\\"TCP\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n# TCP_UDP is the only way to serve TCP and UDP on the same port, since an NLB\\n# permits one listener per port. This is the normal shape for syslog (514/tcp\\n# and 514/udp).\\nresource \\\"aws_lb_listener\\\" \\\"test\\\" {\\n  load_balancer_arn = aws_lb.test.arn\\n  port              = 514\\n  protocol          = \\\"TCP_UDP\\\"\\n\\n  tcp_idle_timeout_seconds = 6000 # ignored\\n\\n  default_action {\\n    type             = \\\"forward\\\"\\n    target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.test.arn\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n</details>\\n\\n\\n### Steps to Reproduce\\n\\n1. `terraform apply` the configuration above.\\n2. Observe that the listener is created and no error or warning mentions\\n   `tcp_idle_timeout_seconds`.\\n3. `aws elbv2 describe-listener-attributes --listener-arn <arn>` \\u2014 `tcp.idle_timeout.seconds` is\\n   `350`, not `6000`. The EC2 console shows the same under the listener's **Attributes** tab.\\n4. `terraform plan` again \\u2014 no diff is reported, so the discrepancy is never surfaced.\\n5. For contrast, change `protocol` to `\\\"TCP\\\"` and re-apply: the attribute is set as configured.\\n\\n\\n### Debug Logging\\n\\n<details open>\\n<summary>Click to expand log output</summary>\\n\\n```console\\nNot captured. The provider makes no attempt to set the attribute for this listener type, so there is\\nno failed API call to log \\u2014 see the code references below.\\n\\n```\\n</details>\\n\\n\\n### GenAI / LLM Assisted Development\\n\\nClaude Code (Claude Opus 5) was used while investigating this behaviour, drafting this report, and\\nwriting the corresponding patch. All findings were verified against the provider source and against\\nlive AWS behaviour before submission.\\n\\n\\n### Important Facts and References\\n\\n**AWS supports the attribute on `TCP_UDP` listeners.** In the EC2 console, a listener shown as\\n`TCP_UDP:514` on an NLB has an **Attributes** tab containing \\\"TCP idle timeout \\u2014 The number of\\nseconds before the listener determines that the TCP connection is idle and closes it\\\", with the\\ndocumented range 60\\u20136000. Editing it to 6000 returns \\\"Attributes successfully updated\\\", and\\n`describe-listener-attributes` then reports 6000. A `TCP_UDP` listener carries TCP flows by\\ndefinition, so a TCP idle timeout is meaningful for it.\\n\\n**And it takes effect, not merely stores.** Before the change, long-lived TCP connections through a\\n`TCP_UDP:514` listener were dropped after every idle period beyond the 350-second default, with the\\nsender learning only when its next write was reset \\u2014 reliably, on a fixed cadence. After setting the\\nattribute to 6000 through the console, connections established afterwards survived idle periods well\\npast 350 seconds and the resets stopped. Existing flows kept the previous timeout until they\\nreconnected, so the value appears to be bound when a flow is established. So the attribute is fully\\nfunctional on this listener type; only the provider declines to manage it.\\n\\n**Code references.** `internal/service/elbv2/listener.go` restricts the attribute to `TCP` and\\n`GENEVE` in two places:\\n\\n```go\\n\\\"tcp_idle_timeout_seconds\\\": {\\n    ...\\n    // Attribute only valid for TCP (NLB) and GENEVE (GWLB) listeners\\n    DiffSuppressFunc: suppressIfListenerProtocolNot(awstypes.ProtocolEnumGeneve, awstypes.ProtocolEnumTcp),\\n},\\n```\\n\\n```go\\n\\\"tcp_idle_timeout_seconds\\\": {\\n    apiAttributeKey:        \\\"tcp.idle_timeout.seconds\\\",\\n    listenerTypesSupported: []awstypes.ProtocolEnum{awstypes.ProtocolEnumTcp, awstypes.ProtocolEnumGeneve},\\n    tfType:                 schema.TypeInt,\\n},\\n```\\n\\nAdding `awstypes.ProtocolEnumTcpUdp` to both lists appears to be the whole fix. The `DiffSuppressFunc`\\nis why the failure is silent rather than an error.\\n\\n**Why it matters in practice.** `TCP_UDP` is the only way to serve TCP and UDP on the same port,\\nbecause an NLB permits one listener per port \\u2014 the standard arrangement for syslog. Those listeners\\nare therefore pinned to the 350-second default with no way to express otherwise in Terraform. An idle\\nNLB flow is discarded silently and the sender is not notified, so for a bursty protocol the first\\nwrite after a quiet period is lost; raising the idle timeout is the documented remedy, and it is\\ncurrently unreachable from configuration for this listener type.\\n\\n**Adjacent confusion worth noting.** `aws_lb`'s `idle_timeout` is documented as \\\"Only valid for Load\\nBalancers of type `application`\\\" and is likewise accepted and ignored when set on an NLB. Two silent\\nno-ops for the same intent, in the same resource pair, makes this behaviour particularly hard to\\ndiagnose \\u2014 a practitioner reasonably concludes the timeout is configured when neither setting has any\\neffect.\\n\\n**Documentation.** `website/docs/r/lb_listener.html.markdown` currently states the argument \\\"Can only\\nbe set if protocol is `TCP` on Network Load Balancer, or with a Gateway Load Balancer\\\", which matches\\nthe code but not the API.\\n\\n**Reference:** [Network Load Balancer listener attributes](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/network/listener-attributes.htm\\nl)\\n\\n\\n### Would you like to implement a fix?\\n\\nYes\\n\",\n7\t  \"author\": \"zzzinn\",\n8\t  \"author_association\": \"NONE\",\n9\t  \"labels\": [\n10\t    \"bug\"\n11\t  ],\n12\t  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-28T17:09:34Z\",\n13\t  \"as_of\": \"2026-09-21T17:22:08Z\"\n14\t}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2884b6b0-3966-4179-951c-301477a1d4f2","uuid":"d94ddb9d-8904-4d65-ac83-62075751e5bf","timestamp":"2026-10-28T17:39:44.881Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/gh-aw/agent/issue.json","content":"{\n  \"number\": 30,\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent/issues/30\",\n  \"repo\": \"wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent\",\n  \"title\": \"[Bug]: aws_lb_listener silently ignores tcp_idle_timeout_seconds on TCP_UDP listeners\",\n  \"body\": \"### Terraform and AWS Provider Version\\n\\n```console\\nTerraform v1.15.2\\non darwin_arm64\\n\\n\\nRuns execute in HCP Terraform, so the provider version is resolved there rather than pinned in the\\nconfiguration.\\n\\nThe Terraform CLI version is incidental: the behaviour is in provider code, and is present in every\\nrelease since the argument was introduced. `tcp_idle_timeout_seconds` first shipped in **v5.73.0**\\n(commit `0b605cb6eca`), and `listenerTypesSupported` has listed only `ProtocolEnumTcp` and\\n`ProtocolEnumGeneve` in v5.73.0, v6.0.0, v6.64.0 and current `main` (`9c81245db08`).\\n`ProtocolEnumTcpUdp` does not appear in the file in any of those.\\n```\\n\\n### Affected Resource(s) or Data Source(s)\\n\\n* `aws_lb_listener`\\n\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nSetting `tcp_idle_timeout_seconds` on a Network Load Balancer listener with `protocol = \\\"TCP_UDP\\\"`\\nconfigures the listener's `tcp.idle_timeout.seconds` attribute,